2026-06-28 14:35:58,143 INFO [stepfun.voice_call] llm full text turn=3 chars=2071 text='Sure! Here’s a little cozy one for you:\nOnce there was a tiny, tattered paper crane that got washed away in a rainstorm, all the way from a little girl’s windowsill to a quiet, mossy forest stream. It drifted for days, past frogs croaking on lily pads, past dragonflies zipping over the water, until it got snagged on a low, gnarled willow branch at the edge of the stream.\nA young badger out digging for worms spotted it first. He gently plucked it free, and when he saw the faint, smudged pencil drawing of a star on one of its wings, he frowned—he knew that star! It was the same star the little girl who lived at the edge of the forest used to draw on rocks near the stream when she came to picnic with her grandma, before she moved away to the city the year before.\nThe badger carried the crane all the way to the old, hollow oak at the center of the forest, where all the forest creatures kept a little lost-and-found nook. One by one, they came to help fix it: a squirrel brought a strip of soft red fabric from an old holiday ribbon to patch its torn wing, a robin brought a tiny drop of pine resin to stick the fabric on, and an old owl brought a small piece of golden mica to glue to the crane’s head, so it would glint like a real star when the sun hit it.\nWhen they were done, they carried the crane all the way to the edge of the forest, right to the spot where the little girl’s grandma used to set up picnic blankets. And just as they set it down on a flat rock, the little girl walked into the forest, visiting for the first time in months. She gasped when she saw the crane, and picked it up, and when she saw the little note the badger had tucked under its wing that said “we kept your star safe for you”, she laughed so hard her eyes crinkled, and she sat on the rock and drew pictures of all the forest creatures for hours.\nAfter that, she came back every weekend, brought little treats for the badger and the squirrel and the robin, and the paper crane sat on her bedroom windowsill, right next to her new drawings of the forest, for years and years.'
