2026-06-28 14:38:33,619 INFO [stepfun.voice_call] llm full text turn=1 chars=2090 text='Oh, I’d love to. Let me tell you a cozy little one, set in a tiny foggy coastal town I’ve always loved in my head:\nOnce upon a time, there was a tiny bakery called The Cinnamon Star, run by a woman named Marnie who’d been kneading dough and frosting cakes there for 40 years. Every Saturday morning, a 7-year-old girl named Lila would stop by after her swim lesson, clutching a crumpled dollar bill she’d saved from her allowance, to buy a single honey oat cookie.\nOne rainy Saturday, Lila showed up soaked through, her pigtails dripping, and told Marnie she’d spent her dollar on a wet, shivering stray cat she’d found huddled in a bush by the library. “I didn’t have any money left for a cookie,” she said, looking down at her scuffed rain boots, “but the cat really looked hungry.”\nMarnie smiled, tucked an extra cookie into a paper bag for Lila, and slipped a small jar of tuna into the bag too for the cat. “No charge for kindness,” she said.\nThat afternoon, when Marnie was closing up the bakery, she found a small, shiny sea glass star on the counter, right where Lila had been standing. It was soft, frosted blue, shaped exactly like a star—she’d never seen anything like it. She tucked it into her apron pocket.\nThe next Saturday, Lila came back with the cat, who she’d named Oat, after the cookie he’d gotten. Oat jumped right onto the counter and nudged a second piece of sea glass toward Marnie—this one was pale pink, also star-shaped.\nAfter that, every week, Lila and Oat would bring Marnie a new piece of star-shaped sea glass they’d found combing the pebble beach. Marnie started hanging them all in the bakery window, and soon, people from all over the coast started stopping by just to see the glittering star display. The bakery got busier, but Marnie always saved a free honey oat cookie for Lila and Oat every Saturday, no charge needed.\nThe story goes that if you stop by The Cinnamon Star on a quiet Saturday, you can still see the sea glass stars twinkling in the window, and if you’re extra kind to someone that day, Marnie might just slip you an extra cookie, too.'
